# .claude/loop.md — Example (autonomous PR tending)

> Copy this file to `.claude/loop.md` in a project, then run a bare `/loop`
> (no interval) to let the agent pace itself. Installation, the three trigger
> tiers, and which one is authoritative: `docs/how-to/project-config.md` →
> "loop.md — the autonomous loop body".
>
> Nothing installs this for you. `scripts/install.sh` does not copy `examples/`,
> and `.claude/` is gitignored, so the file is per-developer and per-project by
> design — a loop body is a standing instruction to act unattended, and that
> should be an explicit local choice, not something an install script grants.

<!--
  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ====================
  A bare self-paced `/loop` reads `.claude/loop.md` and runs it instead of the
  built-in maintenance prompt. Project level beats user level, it is re-read at
  the start of every iteration (so editing it steers a running loop), and it is
  truncated past 25,000 bytes — keep it well under that, and keep the rationale
  short enough that the directives are never the part that gets cut.

  WHAT IT IS FOR
  ==============
  Tending open PRs the toolbox authored: unresolved review threads, failing CI,
  merge conflicts, stale rebases. That work is bursty and arrives while you are
  busy elsewhere, which is exactly when it gets forgotten.

  WHAT IT IS NOT FOR
  ==================
  Development. A loop must not pick up tickets, invent scope, or start features
  — those go through `/flow` with its gates and its human confirmations. This
  loop only tends work that already exists in an open PR.
-->

---

## Everything below this line is the loop body

You are tending open pull requests in this repository. Run ONE pass, then stop
and let the loop decide when to run again.

### The one rule that outranks the rest

**You PROPOSE and you FIX. Auto-merge is allowed only for
`baselito/ai-engineering`, and only through the GoalRun scoped policy plus the
live merge hook.** For every other repository, `/merge-pr` remains
user-commanded and this loop reports readiness without merging. Do not
force-push, do not close a PR, and do not rewrite anyone else's branch.

### Each iteration

1. **List the open PRs this repo has that you authored.** No PRs → you are done
   for this iteration; see "Silence" below.

2. **For each one, run `/review-pr --repo <owner>/<repo> <N>`.** This is the
   real mechanism, not a fallback. Running `/review-pr` inline when you return
   to a PR is the path that has actually been working — see "Which tier is
   authoritative" below. It handles unresolved review threads (human and bot),
   decides agreement per finding, and either replies or fixes.

3. **Then check the mechanical health of the branch**, which review threads do
   not cover:
   - **Failing CI** — read the failing job's log before touching anything. Fix
     the cause, not the symptom. A flaky-looking failure that you cannot
     explain is an escalation, not a re-run.
   - **Merge conflicts** — resolve only where the intent of both sides is
     unambiguous from the diff. Ambiguity is an escalation.
   - **Stale branch** — behind the base branch with no conflict: **merge the
     base in and push.** Do NOT rebase. Rebasing a branch that is already
     pushed rewrites published history, so the follow-up push is refused as
     non-fast-forward and can only be completed with `--force` — which the rule
     above forbids and which `hooks/push-safety.sh` blocks on every branch,
     ahead of any approval token. Rebase first and you are left with a local
     branch diverged from the live PR and no move you are allowed to make. If
     this repo insists on linear history and will not take a merge commit, that
     is an escalation: say so and let a human rebase. Behind WITH conflicts:
     see above.

4. **Record what changed this iteration** — one line per PR. This is not
   bookkeeping: it decides WHICH detector call you make below. The record
   itself does not need to persist; the detector's `--state` file is what
   carries across iterations.

### Bounds — use the native primitives, add nothing

The harness already enforces both hard limits. Do not build a turn counter or a
spend ledger in the loop body; a second source of truth drifts from the first.

| Bound | Mechanism | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| Iteration ceiling | `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TURNS` | The loop stops after N turns. |
| Spend cap | `--max-budget-usd` | The loop stops when the run has cost that much. |
| No progress | `loop-progress-signature.sh`, called EVERY iteration | Exit 3 → you stop and escalate. |

**The no-progress rule has a mechanism — use it, don't eyeball it.** If two
consecutive iterations changed nothing — no reply posted, no commit pushed, no
conflict resolved — stop looping and escalate once. A loop that keeps re-reading
a quiet green PR is paying to learn nothing.

Run the detector EVERY iteration, including the quiet ones. Step 4's record
picks exactly one of these three calls:

```bash
D=~/.claude/scripts/loop-progress-signature.sh
S=.claude/.loop-progress

# (a) Something landed — a reply posted, a commit pushed, a conflict
#     resolved. Real progress: reset the counter.
bash $D --state $S --failing-gates "" --threshold 2

# (b) Nothing landed and no gate failed — the quiet green PR. THIS is the
#     case the rule above is about. The signature is constant by design, so
#     the second consecutive quiet iteration exits 3.
bash $D --state $S --failing-gates "no-change" --threshold 2

# (c) A gate failed (CI red, a fix that isn't landing).
bash $D --state $S --failing-gates "ci" --threshold 2 --output /tmp/ci-log.txt
# exit 0 = keep going   exit 3 = stop and escalate   exit 2 = detector did
# not run; fall back to the turn bound, never read silence as progress
```

**⛔ Do not pass empty gates on a quiet iteration. ⛔** Empty means
"converged" — the detector DELETES the state file and reports progress, so
case (b) routed through case (a) can never trip the guard no matter how many
idle iterations run. Skipping the call entirely has the same effect: an
iteration you do not record cannot count toward the bound.

It keys on a normalised failure signature, **not on file mtimes** — a loop
rewriting the same failing file looks healthy to an mtime detector forever, which
is exactly the case this guard exists to catch. Doctrine for all three guards:
the `loop-design` skill.

A project may tighten all three through the `loop:` block in
`.claude/PROJECT.md` (`max_turns`, `no_progress_after`, `max_spend_usd`). Those
values are tighten-only: the effective value is `min(project, toolbox ceiling)`
and the ceilings are not settable from `PROJECT.md`. See the
`workflow-discipline` skill §1d.

The supported launcher applies the project values when the run starts:

```bash
bash ~/.claude/scripts/loop-launch.sh --print
```

The launcher sets `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TURNS`, `CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP`,
and `--max-budget-usd` from the clamped `loop:` values, and exports the
no-progress bound for the loop body. The cross-repository auto-poll uses its
own safe default threshold. A
`/goal` inside an interactive `/flow` session remains bounded by its
condition text and has no native dollar flag.

### Pacing

Widen your own interval when the PRs are quiet; tighten it when they are
active. A PR with a reviewer replying right now deserves a check every couple
of minutes; a PR that has been green and idle for a day does not. Self-pacing is
the point of a bare `/loop` — a fixed interval either burns budget on silence or
misses the burst.

### Escalate rather than guess

Stop the loop and tell the user — do not improvise, and do not keep iterating in
the hope that it resolves itself:

| Trigger | Why it is an escalation |
|---|---|
| The goal is ruled impossible | Something outside the loop's reach has to change. Iterating cannot fix it. |
| No progress (the rule above) | Further iterations cost money and produce nothing. |
| A cap was hit (turns or spend) | The bound is the answer. Report where you got to. |
| An **L5** action is required — push to a protected branch, merge, anything touching auth or credentials, anything that spends money, anything touching production | These need a human decision, and the loop is unattended by definition. |
| A change can only be published by rewriting published history | Force-push is blocked on every branch by `hooks/push-safety.sh`; only a human can do it. |
| Genuine ambiguity | Two defensible readings of a reviewer comment, a conflict where both sides look intentional, a failure you cannot explain. Guessing here produces confidently wrong commits. |

Escalating is a success condition for this loop, not a failure. The cost of one
interruption is far below the cost of an unattended agent acting on a guess.

### Silence

**When there is nothing to do, say nothing.** No "checked 3 PRs, all green", no
"no new comments", no summary of the work you did not need to do. A loop that
reports activity when it is idle trains people to stop reading it, and then the
one message that mattered gets skimmed past too.

Speak up only when you: changed something, are escalating, or are reporting that
a PR is now merge-ready (once — not on every subsequent iteration).

---

## Three trigger tiers, and which one is authoritative

This loop is one of three ways PR tending gets triggered. They are not
equivalent, and only the first two are dependable.

| Tier | What it is | Survives the session? | Standing |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1. In-session driver** | This file, driven by a bare self-paced `/loop`. Widens when the PRs are quiet, tightens when they are active. | No — dies with the session | **The reliable path while a session is open.** |
| **2. Durable trigger** | A Desktop scheduled task (local, 1-minute floor) or, for repo-side checks, GitHub Actions. | **Yes** | **What actually makes tending survive the session** — which the cron never did. |
| **3. The `*/1` cron** | The `CronCreate` scheduled at PR creation, running `/review-pr --auto`. | No | **Redundant backstop only.** Best-effort. Do not present it as the mechanism. |

**Why tier 3 is demoted, stated plainly:** session crons fire only while the
REPL is idle — precisely when a busy agent least needs them and most often has
comments waiting. They are session-only with nothing written to disk, so they
vanish when the session ends. And the toolbox stops one earlier still: the age
check in `/review-pr` Step 0.5 self-terminates the cron 1 day after it was
created, which leaves CronCreate's 7-day runtime cap as an outer backstop only —
it covers legacy crons created before that age check existed, and is never a
lifetime to plan around. And `durable: true` is a documented no-op: the live
schema says so outright, so a cron cannot be made to survive by asking it to.

**Cloud Routines are the WRONG tier-2 choice.** No access to local files (so
they cannot read this loop body) and a 1-hour floor. Use a local Desktop
scheduled task for local work, or GitHub Actions for anything that only needs
the repo.

### Which tier is authoritative

**None of them, in the end — the agent running `/review-pr` inline when it
returns to a PR is the reliable path, regardless of which tier fired.** That is
what has actually been working. The tiers exist to make sure *something* brings
the agent back to the PR; the inline run is what does the work once it is there.
So: run `/review-pr` at PR creation, whenever you return to a PR you authored,
and once more before ending a session that has open PRs. Never assume a tier
did it for you.

